::CW_U01_L02_GP_Q01::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Passage: "The trophy leaned in the case, dusty and forgotten, while Marcus walked past without slowing down." Which tone is created most clearly?{
~A. Playful#Incorrect. The words "dusty," "forgotten," and "without slowing down" do not create a playful attitude.
=B. Detached#Correct. The narrator presents the trophy and Marcus with emotional distance, creating a detached tone.
~C. Celebratory#Incorrect. A celebratory tone would emphasize pride or excitement, not neglect.
~D. Angry#Incorrect. The passage does not use intense diction or direct frustration.
}
::CW_U01_L02_GP_Q02::[MLA.CW.WR.05] Passage: "Of course the elevator stopped between floors, because apparently my day needed a plot twist." Which craft term best describes the speaker's word choice and attitude?{
~A. Setting#Incorrect. The elevator is part of the setting, but the question asks about word choice and attitude.
~B. Dialogue#Incorrect. No character conversation appears in the passage.
=C. Diction#Correct. The phrase "apparently my day needed a plot twist" is a diction choice that helps create voice and tone.
~D. Transition#Incorrect. A transition shows movement between ideas or events.
}
::CW_U01_L02_GP_Q03::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Passage: "The envelope waited on the desk. No return address. No stamp. Just my name, written in blue ink I recognized immediately." What is the writer's likely purpose?{
~A. To create a humorous scene#Incorrect. The short fragments and unexplained envelope create tension, not humor.
~B. To summarize a full school day#Incorrect. The passage focuses on one mysterious object, not a full day.
~C. To explain postal rules#Incorrect. The missing stamp supports mystery, not an informational purpose.
=D. To create suspense and curiosity#Correct. The writer uses fragments and withheld information to make the reader wonder about the envelope.
}
::CW_U01_L02_GP_Q04::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Passage: "I should have turned back when the hallway lights flickered, but curiosity has never been one of my safer qualities." Which point of view is used?{
=A. First person#Correct. The narrator uses "I" and "my," which shows first-person point of view.
~B. Second person#Incorrect. Second person would address "you."
~C. Third-person limited#Incorrect. Third person would use names or pronouns such as he, she, or they.
~D. Third-person omniscient#Incorrect. The passage is told by an "I" narrator, not an all-knowing narrator.
}
::CW_U01_L02_GP_Q05::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Passage: "The kitten attacked the shoelace with the seriousness of a tiny knight defending a kingdom." Which revision would make the tone more serious?{
~A. The kitten declared war on the shoelace kingdom.#Incorrect. This keeps a playful, exaggerated tone.
~B. The kitten was super funny with the shoelace.#Incorrect. This directly creates a humorous tone.
~C. The kitten performed a heroic battle against the lace.#Incorrect. This still sounds playful and dramatic.
=D. The kitten gripped the shoelace tightly and would not let go.#Correct. This removes the comic comparison and uses more direct, serious diction.
}
